const body = `<h1>2.1 — Coins: Triggers, Tags &amp; Your First Stat</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your character can walk, but it cannot <em>touch</em> anything. Walk straight over the coin
    and nothing happens — not because your code is wrong, but because nothing in this entire
    project is built to notice a collision. Today you make the first object in the game that
    reacts to the player, and in doing so you switch the physics engine on for good.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I detect a collision, and make something happen?', 1)}

${renderDecompose('2.1', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Your goal: make a coin disappear when the player touches it.</strong> Before
        you write a single line, answer this honestly — how would Unity ever <em>know</em> the
        two objects touched? The Player has a Transform. The Coin has a Transform. Two
        positions in space do not collide. Nothing in the engine is checking.
    </p>
    <p>
        Something has to tell it what <em>shape</em> each object is, and something has to be
        simulated every frame so those shapes can be tested against each other. Read what Unity
        gives you:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Component</th><th>What it contributes</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Collider2D</code></td>
            <td>Gives an object a <strong>shape</strong> the physics engine can test against other shapes. Without one, an object is invisible to physics — it is only a picture.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Rigidbody2D</code></td>
            <td>Puts an object <strong>under simulation</strong>: the engine tracks it every physics step, and it is what makes overlap messages fire at all.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Is Trigger</code> (checkbox on any Collider2D)</td>
            <td>Changes what an overlap <em>means</em>. A solid collider pushes objects apart. A trigger does not push — it only <strong>reports</strong> that the overlap happened.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code></td>
            <td>The message your script receives on the first frame two shapes overlap. This is the event a coin pickup is built on.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>CompareTag</code></td>
            <td>How you check <em>what</em> you just touched. Asks the engine whether a collider carries a given tag.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>The rule that catches everyone.</strong> Unity's 2D physics only simulates
        objects that have a <strong>Collider2D</strong>, and overlap messages only fire when
        <strong>at least one of the two objects also has a Rigidbody2D</strong>. Two colliders
        with no body anywhere produce <em>silence</em> — no error, no warning, nothing to debug.
        <br><br>
        And here is the part that matters today: in this project <strong>nothing in
        <code>Assets/Prefabs</code> has either one — except the Switch</strong>. Not the Player.
        Not the Coin. Not the Spikes. You are about to be the first person to give anything in
        this game a physics body.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: What Is a Collider For?</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>A Transform stores position, rotation and scale. What does a Collider2D store that a Transform has no way to express?</li>
            <li>The physics engine runs on its own schedule, not inside your <code>Update()</code>. Why would Unity be built that way, instead of letting your script test for overlaps once a frame?</li>
            <li>A trigger pushes nothing — it only reports. Name two game behaviours besides a coin pickup where a report-only overlap is the right tool.</li>
            <li>A collider is a shape; a rigidbody is a thing under simulation. The Player is the object that moves. Which of the two must live on the Player for a coin to ever notice it, and which one <em>could</em> live on the coin instead?</li>
            <li>The minimum is "a collider on both, a body on one". Which object should carry the body — the coin that sits still and vanishes, or the player that moves all game? Argue it, then build it that way.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">What You Just Switched On</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>A body changes the whole game — go and see what you broke.</strong>
        The moment the Player has a Collider2D and a Rigidbody2D, <em>every</em> collider in the
        scene starts interacting with it. That includes the <strong>Cave Entrances</strong>,
        which carry solid colliders wired to a scene-switching script that has been sitting
        dormant this whole time, precisely because your Player had no collider to trip it.
        <br><br>
        So: walk into a cave mouth and find out what happens. If you are suddenly standing in a
        different scene, <strong>nothing is broken — you switched the physics engine on.</strong>
        Write down what happened and why. Do <em>not</em> fix it yet, and do not delete the cave
        entrances: Unit 5 builds the cave properly, and this is the first thread of it.
    </div>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>One number, one home.</strong> For now the coin count lives on
        <code>PlayerController</code>. There is a <code>StatManager.cs</code> in
        <code>Assets/Scripts</code> — it is empty, and it stays empty. Later in the year you will
        have a real reason to move your stats into a manager, and that reason will come from a
        scene change, not from tidiness. Until then, every stat you build belongs to the player.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: Where Should a Stat Live?</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        You just made a real design decision without being told to. Here are the three honest
        options for where a number like "coins" can live, and what each one costs:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Approach</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>What it costs</th><th>When it wins</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(a) A field on <code>PlayerController</code></strong></td>
            <td><code>[SerializeField] private int coins;</code></td>
            <td>The player owns the number, so anything else that wants it must find the player first.</td>
            <td><strong>Now.</strong> The only thing that touches coins <em>is</em> the player.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(b) A separate <code>PlayerStats</code> component</strong></td>
            <td>The numbers live in a component whose only job is numbers.</td>
            <td>One more component, one more reference to cache, one more thing to keep in sync.</td>
            <td>When <code>PlayerController</code> grows long enough that movement code and stat code start fighting over the same file.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(c) A global singleton <code>StatManager</code></strong></td>
            <td>Any script, anywhere, reads the numbers from one shared object.</td>
            <td>Everything can reach it, so nothing protects it — and to be worth having at all, it must survive a scene change.</td>
            <td>When several systems need the same numbers <em>and</em> those numbers must outlive a scene load.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Where Does a Number Belong?</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Which of the three did you just build, and which line of your own code proves it?</li>
            <li>Open <code>Assets/Scripts/StatManager.cs</code> and read it. If you filled it in as a singleton today, would your coin count survive a scene change? Would that be a feature or a problem <em>right now</em>?</li>
            <li>The coin disappears when collected, but "how many coins I have" is not a property of the coin. So where does that number really belong — the object that vanishes, or the object that persists?</li>
            <li>Which option makes the number easiest to find when you reopen this project in three weeks, and which makes it easiest to test on its own?</li>
            <li>You just watched a cave entrance teleport you somewhere. What does that tell you about whether your stats need to survive a scene change <em>this month</em>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Bug Hunt &amp; Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Tracing Pickup Bugs</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Untick <strong>Is Trigger</strong> on the coin's collider. Does <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> still run? What happens instead, and where does the player end up?</li>
            <li>Remove the Rigidbody2D from the Player but leave every collider in place. Does the coin still vanish? Does the Console show a message, or nothing at all?</li>
            <li>Change your destroy call to <code>Destroy(gameObject)</code> and collect a coin. Which object disappeared — and what is the Console still printing that tells you the player survived?</li>
            <li>Comment out the destroy line and walk in and out of the same coin five times. What does the count say? Why is a coin that pays out every time a bug rather than a bonus?</li>
            <li>Set the Player's Gravity Scale back to 1. What does the character do when you stop pressing keys, and which setting was quietly doing the work all along?</li>
            <li>Move your <code>coins = coins + 1;</code> line up so it sits <em>before</em> the <code>if</code> check instead of inside it. Walk into a wall. What does the count do, and what does that tell you about what the <code>if</code> is actually guarding?</li>
            <li>Walk into a Cave Entrance deliberately with your new body and collider. Which two components you added are responsible — and given the tag check inside that script, why did it react to <em>you</em> and not to a wall?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Destroy versus SetActive</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Replace <code>Destroy(other.gameObject)</code> with <code>other.gameObject.SetActive(false)</code>. What changes on screen, what is different in the Hierarchy <em>after</em> you stop playing, and what happens to your count?</li>
            <li>Read the docs for <code>SetActive</code>. In a game with thousands of coins, why might switching an object off and reusing it beat destroying it and building a new one?</li>
            <li>Open <code>Assets/Scripts/Package Scripts/SceneSwitch.cs</code> and find the method that fires when the player touches a cave mouth. Which parameter type does it take, and which of the two components you added today made it fire?</li>
            <li>Print your coin count with <code>Debug.Log</code> every frame instead of only on pickup. What happens to the Console, and why was printing on the event the better choice?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
